      <section class="features wrap">
        <h2>What the mesh gives you</h2>
        <div class="grid">
          {
            FEATURES.map((f) => (
              <a class="card" href={f.href}>
                <span class="num">{f.num}</span>
                <h3>{f.title}</h3>
                <p>{f.body}</p>
              </a>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="how wrap">
        <h2>How it works</h2>
        <div class="strip">
          <div class="step">
            <span class="num">01</span>
            <h3>Gateway</h3>
            <p>
              Every pod runs a per-pod gateway — the agent process manager that
              owns spawn, registration, and lifecycle.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <span class="num">02</span>
            <h3>Route</h3>
            <p>
              The router moves A2A wire traffic between agents across the mesh:
              task, event, status, and model routes.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <span class="num">03</span>
            <h3>Memory</h3>
            <p>
              A temporal memory contract keeps team context durable across
              ephemeral agent lifecycles.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="hosts wrap">
        <h2>Hosts, not owners</h2>
        <div class="pane">
          <h3>Keep your control plane</h3>
          <p>
            The module owns the mechanism — gateways, routing, lifecycle,
            registration seams, the event contract — and nothing else. Scheduling
            decisions, identity, telemetry backends and memory stores stay
            host-side, plugged in behind the seams.
          </p>
          <p>
            k8squad is host #1: it pins the module with its role-based
            scheduling running as SchedulingPolicy #1, and its CRDs project into
            the mesh contracts rather than being replaced by them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href={`${BASE}concepts/hosts/`}>What the module owns vs. what stays host-side →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="how wrap">
        <h2>Get started in three steps</h2>
        <div class="strip">
          <div class="step">
            <span class="num">01</span>
            <h3>Create a cluster</h3>
            <p>
              Spin up a fresh kind cluster — the quickstart's zero-host-project
              path needs nothing but a container runtime.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <span class="num">02</span>
            <h3>Install the chart</h3>
            <p>
              One helm install of the vanilla chart — no host project, no
              dependencies, fully independent.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <span class="num">03</span>
            <h3>Run your first task</h3>
            <p>
              POST a task to the gateway and watch the A2A NDJSON event stream
              settle to a terminal state.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="start-cta">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`${BASE}docs/quickstart/`}>
            Open the quickstart
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>

